[{:db/id 17592186128976 :question/problem "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix ![](images/11/1198325c2b7aeab6d1c4e5f405f1e979d408c4c8f128851f039202d4fa94c166.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/97/97e30eb12530173f3d4b7f90aa3dbbf9cef187ef33154f4da600a393c5777b43.svg)\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129159 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-408fe19d-d084-5aed-90d7-346f39925c23"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-408fe19d-d084-5aed-90d7-346f39925c23" :question/id #uuid "408fe19d-d084-5aed-90d7-346f39925c23" :question/math-academy-id "q-81181" :question/problem "Given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+3\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$ are eigenvalues of a real $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $\\frac{1}{4}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=4-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}((4+3\\text{i})+(4-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e4f4638-8952-5dfa-8fd2-e5c0fe083b3a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e4f4638-8952-5dfa-8fd2-e5c0fe083b3a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4f4638-8952-5dfa-8fd2-e5c0fe083b3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9f63ef42-06cc-58ad-b830-813ec429fb50" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d29b5d3e-3568-573d-86cb-207f80fa38a7" "answer-dba03e7d-2a34-55c7-a14a-96455d27dcd9" "answer-9f63ef42-06cc-58ad-b830-813ec429fb50" "answer-20b4f533-7424-5726-bf17-08f22b518fc4" "answer-1b833791-e910-521c-87da-41147c8cc0ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d29b5d3e-3568-573d-86cb-207f80fa38a7" :answer/id #uuid "d29b5d3e-3568-573d-86cb-207f80fa38a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dba03e7d-2a34-55c7-a14a-96455d27dcd9" :answer/id #uuid "dba03e7d-2a34-55c7-a14a-96455d27dcd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f63ef42-06cc-58ad-b830-813ec429fb50" :answer/id #uuid "9f63ef42-06cc-58ad-b830-813ec429fb50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20b4f533-7424-5726-bf17-08f22b518fc4" :answer/id #uuid "20b4f533-7424-5726-bf17-08f22b518fc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b833791-e910-521c-87da-41147c8cc0ef" :answer/id #uuid "1b833791-e910-521c-87da-41147c8cc0ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130351 :tutorial/content "Let's compute the eigenvalues of the following matrix:\n\n![](images/1b/1bc3c21f31a351f7f7281824a0d6db9fce0c1581f6506e6533c2352fe6460788.svg)\n\nThe characteristic equation of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +1 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic equation. However, the discriminant of this equation is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nso $A$ doesn't have any real eigenvalues! \n\nNonetheless, we can still compute the eigenvalues of $A.$ The difference here is that they will be complex numbers. Let's solve the characteristic equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}=-1 \\\\ \\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda = \\pm\\textrm{i}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130352 :tutorial/content "You may have noticed that if $\\lambda$ is a complex eigenvalue of a real matrix $A,$ then the complex conjugate $\\overline{\\lambda}$ also appears to be an eigenvalue.\n\nMoreover, if the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ corresponds to an eigenvector $\\mathbb{v},$ then the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}$ corresponds to an eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbb v}.$\n\nAs a matter of fact, this is true for all matrices with real entries:\n\nLet $A$ be a matrix with real entries. If $A$ has an eigenvector $\\mathbb v$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ then $A$ also has an eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbb v}$ with eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}.$\n\nLet's prove this. If a real matrix $A$ has an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ then\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}\t=\t\\lambda\\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nTaking the complex conjugate of both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{Av} & =\\overset{―}{\\lambda v} \\\\ \\overset{―}{A}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\overset{―}{\\lambda }\\overset{―}{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut since $A$ is a matrix with real entries, we have that $\\overline{A} = A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA\\overline{\\mathbf{v}} = \\overline{\\lambda}\\overline{\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\overline{\\lambda}$ is also an eigenvalue of $A$ and its corresponding eigenvector is $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129359 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
